"Around the year 125 a student named Arrian wrote down a short summary of what he had heard from his teacher, a freed slave who ran a school in a provincial Greek town. The handbook begins: There are things which are within our power, and there are things which are beyond our power. Within our power are opinion, aim, desire, aversion, and, in one word, whatever affairs are our own. Beyond our power are body, property, reputation, office, and, in one word, whatever are not properly our own affairs. The teacher was Epictetus, and the rest of his philosophy follows from the division. What is up to us is free and cannot be hindered. What is not up to us belongs to others, and whoever sets his heart on it will lament and find fault both with gods and men.": "1d3caf2d0839",
"Everything has two handles: one by which it may be borne, another by which it cannot. If your brother acts unjustly, do not lay hold on the affair by the handle of his injustice, for by that it cannot be borne, but rather by the opposite—that he is your brother, that he was brought up with you; and thus you will lay hold on it as it is to be borne.": "540c9568dfca",
"He credited his survival to the first sentence of the handbook.": "1548f3b5e327",
"In September 1965 the American pilot James Stockdale was shot down over North Vietnam. He had studied philosophy at Stanford a few years earlier, and a professor had given him the handbook of Epictetus. As he drifted down under his parachute, he later wrote, he said to himself that he was leaving the world of technology and entering the world of Epictetus. He was a prisoner for seven and a half years, four of them in solitary confinement, and was tortured repeatedly. He credited his survival to the first sentence of the handbook. His captors controlled his body, his food, and his news of home. They did not control what he would agree to, and he organized the other prisoners around that fact. He noticed that the men who broke were often the optimists, who told themselves they would be home by Christmas, and were crushed when Christmas passed.": "e2f1424d3241",
"In one word, whatever affairs are our own.": "5c37b8af5428",
"Lay hold on it as it is to be borne.": "cc18e7650c28",
"Men are disturbed not by things, but by the views which they take of things. Thus death is nothing terrible, else it would have appeared so to Socrates. But the terror consists in our notion of death, that it is terrible. When, therefore, we are hindered or disturbed, or grieved, let us never impute it to others, but to ourselves—that is, to our own views.": "a6c7a058d285",
"Mencius drew the same line four centuries before Epictetus, in two sentences. Seek and you will get it, let go and you will lose it: here seeking helps the getting, because what is sought lies within myself. There is a way to seek it, and getting it depends on fate: here seeking does not help the getting, because what is sought lies outside. What lies within, for Mencius, is the cultivation of ones own character, and what lies outside is wealth, rank, and length of life. Confucius described his own attitude in six characters: I do not resent Heaven, and I do not blame men. The Confucians gave the outside a name, mìng, what is decreed, and they taught that a person does everything that is his to do and then awaits the decree.": "f0db455783a8",
"None of it would come as a surprise.": "06ea51f757f6",
"Notice what is on the second list. The body is there. A man can train and eat well and still fall ill. Reputation is there, since it lives in other peoples minds. Even ones own life is there. What remains on the first list is small and of a single kind: our judgments, and the wants and choices that follow from them.": "d1959f65d86f",
"Stoicism": "f9347a205237",
"Tears come of themselves.": "d5f8b654657c",
"The Buddha makes the Stoic point with an image. When an untrained person is touched by a painful feeling, he grieves and laments. Its like a person who is struck with an arrow, only to be struck with a second arrow. The first arrow is the pain. The second is the minds reaction to it, the resistance and the story of how unbearable it is. A trained person feels the first arrow and is not struck by the second. This is Epictetuss distinction between the thing and the view, located where the Stoics also located it, in the moment between feeling and reaction. The Buddha likewise lists the conditions that toss people about: gain and loss, fame and disgrace, blame and praise, pleasure and pain. They are called the eight worldly winds, and every item belongs on Epictetuss second list.": "86209489c0b8",
"The emperor Marcus Aurelius, who read Epictetus and kept a notebook of exercises for his own use, put it this way: If thou art pained by any external thing, it is not this thing that disturbs thee, but thy own judgement about it. And it is in thy power to wipe out this judgement now. He began his days by reminding himself that he would meet with ingratitude, arrogance, and deceit, so that none of it would come as a surprise.": "e9a37e6cb592",
"The method has also been tested in clinics. The psychologists who founded cognitive behavioral therapy in the 1950s and 1960s, Albert Ellis and Aaron Beck, both cited Epictetus as a source, and the therapys central technique is his: identify the judgment that stands between an event and the distress, and examine whether it is true. It is among the best-supported treatments for anxiety and depression.": "992e3cf6f238",
"The outcome is none of your concern.": "0a6fd97e9f72",
"The second step is a claim about where suffering comes from. Men are disturbed not by things, but by the views which they take of things. Death is not terrible in itself, Epictetus argues, or it would have seemed so to Socrates. The terror lies in our opinion that it is terrible. When someone insults you, what hurts is your own view that you have been harmed. Between the event and the distress there is always a judgment, and the judgment is ours to make. Stoic training consists in catching that judgment before it has been made. An impression arrives: I have been wronged. The student learns to say to it, You are only an appearance, and to examine it before agreeing.": "cf519c9da18c",
"The third doubt is moral. A philosophy that teaches people to change their desires and not their circumstances suits the powerless, and it also suits those who would like the powerless to stay quiet. Epictetus tells a man to remind himself, when he kisses his child, that he is kissing a mortal, so that he will not be shattered if the child dies. Many readers feel that something has gone wrong when grief is treated as an error of judgment. The Stoic reply is that one can love fully and still know what one loves. Whether that is wisdom or a refusal to be fully in ones life is the question the school has always had to face.": "6ece5e2d26e4",
"The two part company on what does the controlling. For Epictetus the faculty of judgment and choice is the true self, a fragment of divine reason, and the whole of ethics is to keep it free. The Buddha finds no such commander. Judgments and intentions are themselves conditioned events, and a person gains freedom by seeing them clearly and by long training of attention, with no inner citadel to defend. They differ also in what calm is for. Stoic calm rests on the belief that the world is governed for the best. Buddhist equanimity makes no such claim about the world, and it is listed beside loving-kindness and compassion as one of four states to be cultivated together, so that it does not harden into indifference (Dharma and the West, D20 and D22).": "599af3ab0fa6",
"They were crushed when Christmas passed.": "152ed60c9eb7",
"Three doubts are worth carrying into the exercise. The first is whether the division is too sharp. Epictetus has two lists, and most of life seems to fall between them. My health, my childs character, and whether I win the match are things I influence and do not determine. A modern Stoic, William Irvine, proposes a third category for these and a rule for handling them: set your goal in terms of what is yours. Aim to play your best game, since winning is not up to you. The second doubt is whether the first list is as secure as Epictetus thought. Our judgments are shaped by mood, fatigue, upbringing, and illness, and a person in a deep depression cannot simply wipe one out.": "c28988724280",
"What should one want, then? Epictetus answers with an instruction that sounds like surrender: Demand not that events should happen as you wish; but wish them to happen as they do happen, and you will go on well. His image is the theater. Remember that you are an actor in a drama of such sort as the Author chooses. If it be his pleasure that you should enact a poor man, or a cripple, or a ruler, or a private citizen, see that you act it well. For this is your business—to act well the given part, but to choose it belongs to another. He was himself lame, and had been a slave. The Stoics believed that the universe is governed by a rational providence, so that whatever happens is part of a good order. To want things otherwise is to quarrel with the nature of things. The teaching is not passivity. The actor works hard at the part. An archer does everything to aim well and then lets the arrow go, and whether it hits is no longer his affair.": "356f163fddf5",
"Whoever sets his heart on it will lament.": "1d677d7d453b",
"You have staked your peace on things that can be taken from you.": "e5513c5ba81d",
"You will find fault both with gods and men.": "495868dc3912",
"Zhuangzi states the conclusion in its most extreme form: To know what cannot be helped and to rest in it as in fate is the perfection of virtue. The resemblance to the Stoics is close, and the two traditions arose with no contact. The similarity of their situations may explain it. Both were philosophies for people living under powers they could not influence, an emperor in one case and the warring kings in the other.": "ba751e3bc469",
